 ## Tests
 
+`File.readTextIn`, `readBytesIn`, `writeTextIn`, `appendTextIn`, `writeBytesIn`, and `appendBytesIn` take a checked `Scope.PathIn`. Their `File.Read` or `File.Write` effect uses the same range as that input type. They operate on the path retained by the check; the range describes path strings and does not isolate symbolic-link targets or special files. A complete `File.Read` handler needs all 13 operations, including both new read operations; a complete `File.Write` handler needs all 16 operations, including the four new write operations. The examples below replace one operation and keep the remaining effect in `uses`.
+
+Write a projection or widening call directly on the right-hand side of `bind` before using its result in another expression. A narrow handler argument has no local check provenance, so this form still cannot project or widen that argument. Forward it with the same narrow input type, or use a bare `Scope.PathIn` input when projection is needed; IO through the bare type has a Top range.
+
 A test is a function with `@test("description")`, no parameters, and the return type `Unit` or `Result[Unit, String]` (use the latter when the test uses `try e or return`). Check with `Assert.equal(actual: actual, expected: expected)`. Replace an operation such as `File.readText` or `Process.run` with a handler so that the test does not touch the outside world; more examples are in `references/idioms.md`. Checks in generic helpers recover constructor names when the caller's types can be determined; otherwise, a type may remain unknown. If a report shows `<constructor #n of an unknown type>` (the zero-based constructor position), compare values with a concrete type in the check to show their names.
 
